      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "STUDY OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To compare the intraoperative and postoperative clinical properties of the sciatic nerve block performed with either 0.5% bupivacaine or 0.5% levobupivacaine for orthopedic foot procedures."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Randomized, double-blind study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "Inpatient unit of a university-affiliated hospital."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS",
          "content": "30 ASA physical status I and II patients undergoing elective hallux valgus repair under regional anesthesia."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTIONS",
          "content": "After administering intravenous (IV) midazolam premedication (0.05 mg/kg), a femoral nerve block was performed with 15 mL of mepivacaine 2%. Patients were then randomly allocated to receive, in a double-blind fashion, a sciatic nerve block with 20 mL of either 0.5% bupivacaine (n = 15) or 0.5% levobupivacaine (n = 15)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "MEASUREMENTS AND MAIN RESULTS",
          "content": "An observer who was blinded to the study drug recorded the onset time, quality, and duration of the sciatic nerve block. Postoperative analgesia consisted of 100 mg IV ketoprofen every 8 hours, with the first administration given at the patient's request. Mean (+/-SEM) onset time of the sciatic nerve block was 35 +/- 5 minutes for bupivacaine and 31 +/- 6 minutes for levobupivacaine (p = not significant [NS]). The duration of motor and sensory blocks with bupivacaine was 761 +/- 112 minutes and 790 +/- 110 minutes, respectively, and 716 +/- 80 minutes and 814 +/- 73 minutes, respectively, with levobupivacaine (p = NS). The first pain medication was requested after 844 +/- 96 minutes with bupivacaine and 872 +/- 75 minutes after levobupivacaine (p = NS). No differences in the quality of nerve block and patient satisfaction were reported between the two groups."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "A dose of 20 mL of 0.5% levobupivacaine induces sciatic nerve block of similar onset, duration, and intensity as the block produced by the same volume and concentration of the racemic solution of bupivacaine."
        }
      ]},
